---
description: How to behave when asked to modify or extend an already-built site
alwaysApply: true
---

- When asked to modify an existing section, only touch the files relevant to that
  section — do not refactor unrelated components as a side effect of an unrelated request.
- Before declaring a change complete, mentally re-check it against the relevant rule
  files above (design, responsive, animation, accessibility, images, whatsapp, forms,
  seo) and note in your response what you'd manually verify (e.g. "test this at 375px
  and with prefers-reduced-motion on").
- If a requested change conflicts with an accessibility rule, a content-accuracy rule,
  or the single-source-of-truth rule (e.g. "just hardcode this number for now"), flag
  the conflict and explain why, rather than silently overriding the rule.
- When adding a brand-new field to the site (e.g. an insurance-partners logo strip),
  add it to `site-data.json` and `site-data-types.ts` first, then build the component
  to consume it — never hardcode the new content directly to save a step.
- When onboarding a new client onto this same codebase, the only files that should
  change are `/content/site-data.json` and the contents of `/public/images` — if a
  change request seems to require touching component code just to rebrand for a new
  client, that's a sign something was hardcoded earlier and should be fixed at the
  source instead.
